.SH "Detailed Description"
.PP 
Network targets allow logs to be sent over a network to a remote log collector or relay\&. 

There are a number of popular options for the remote end including Splunk, rsyslog, and syslog-ng\&. Network targets can send messages to these and others, over both IPv4 and IPv6, TCP and UDP\&.
.PP
\fBThread Safety: MT-Safe\fP Logging to network targets is thread safe by virtue of using the network logging functions which are thread safe themselves\&.
.PP
\fBAsync Signal Safety: AS-Unsafe lock\fP Logging to network targets is not signal safe, as a non-reentrant lock is used to coordinate the read of the entry with other potential accesses\&.
.PP
\fBAsync Cancel Safety: AC-Unsafe lock\fP Logging to network targets is not safe to call from threads that may be asynchronously cancelled, as the cleanup of the lock used for entries may not be completed\&. 
.SH "Macro Definition Documentation"
.PP 
.SS "#define STUMPLESS_DEFAULT_TRANSPORT_PORT   '514'"

.PP
The default transport port for network targets\&. This is based largely on tradition, but some applications may expect a different port (for example 1514 and 6514 are sometimes used)\&. If a different port is needed on a target, use \fBstumpless_set_transport_port()\fP to change it\&. 
.SS "#define STUMPLESS_DEFAULT_UDP_MAX_MESSAGE_SIZE   1472"

.PP
The default message size for UDP network targets\&. This is set to account for an MTU of 1500 bytes, a 20 byte IP header, and an 8 byte datagram header\&. If you wish to change this value for a particular target, then you must use the \fBstumpless_set_udp_max_message_size()\fP function on the target\&. 
