.SS "struct \fBstumpless_target\fP * stumpless_open_network_target (const char * name, const char * destination, enum \fBstumpless_network_protocol\fP network, enum \fBstumpless_transport_protocol\fP transport)"

.PP
Opens a network target\&. Network targets allow traditional syslog messages to be sent to a remote server\&. This function allows the type of network target to be specified by parameters, but if you know that you want a specific type then it is easier to use the simplified functions that specify the type by name, such as the stumpless_open_udp4_target function\&.
.PP
\fBThread Safety: MT-Safe race:name race:destination\fP This function is thread safe, of course assuming that name and destination are not modified by any other threads during execution\&.
.PP
\fBAsync Signal Safety: AS-Unsafe heap\fP This function is not safe to call from signal handlers due to the use of memory allocation functions\&.
.PP
\fBAsync Cancel Safety: AC-Unsafe heap\fP This function is not safe to call from threads that may be asynchronously cancelled, as the memory allocation function may not be AC-Safe itself\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIname\fP The name of the target to open\&. This is only used for identification of the target\&.
.br
\fIdestination\fP The destination to send the messages to\&. This could be a hostname or an IP address\&.
.br
\fInetwork\fP The network protocol to use\&.
.br
\fItransport\fP The transport protocol to use\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
The opened target if no error is encountered\&. In the event of an error, NULL is returned and an error code is set appropriately\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "struct \fBstumpless_target\fP * stumpless_open_tcp4_target (const char * name, const char * destination)"

.PP
Opens a network target for remote logging over IPv4 and TCP\&. A TCP4 target will use Syslog over TCP, as defined in RFC 6587\&. Note that this includes the use of octet counting for transmission of messages, instead of a delimiter such as a newline character\&.
.PP
\fBThread Safety: MT-Safe race:name race:destination\fP This function is thread safe, of course assuming that name and destination is not modified by any other threads during execution\&.
.PP
\fBAsync Signal Safety: AS-Unsafe heap\fP This function is not safe to call from signal handlers due to the use of memory allocation functions\&.
.PP
\fBAsync Cancel Safety: AC-Unsafe heap\fP This function is not safe to call from threads that may be asynchronously cancelled, as the memory allocation function may not be AC-Safe itself\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIname\fP The name of the target to open\&. This is only used for identification of the target\&.
.br
\fIdestination\fP The destination to send the messages to\&. This could be a hostname or an IP address\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
The opened target if no error is encountered\&. In the event of an error, NULL is returned and an error code is set appropriately\&. 
.RE
.PP

