.SS "struct \fBstumpless_target\fP * stumpless_set_udp_max_message_size (struct \fBstumpless_target\fP * target, size_t max_msg_size)"

.PP
Sets the maximum message size of a UDP network target\&. Messages that are longer than the maximum size are truncated to this length\&. If the underlying transport for a network target can support a larger datagram and you need support for longer messages, then you can use this option to increase the size\&. Likewise, the option can be used to force smaller datagrams if needed, for example in the case of extra overhead in the IP header beyond the typical 20 bytes\&.
.PP
Note that truncations due to the maximum size do not take UTF characters into account\&. If you have entries using multi-byte characters, then you need to be sure that these truncations do not cause parsing or security problems\&.
.PP
Without calling this function, UDP targets start with a maximum message size set to \fCSTUMPLESS_DEFAULT_UDP_MAX_MESSAGE_SIZE\fP\&.
.PP
\fBThread Safety: MT-Safe\fP This function is thread safe\&. A mutex is used to coordinate changes to the target while it is being modified\&.
.PP
\fBAsync Signal Safety: AS-Unsafe lock\fP This function is not safe to call from signal handlers due to the use of a non-reentrant lock to coordinate changes\&.
.PP
\fBAsync Cancel Safety: AC-Unsafe lock\fP This function is not safe to call from threads that may be asynchronously cancelled, due to the use of a lock that could be left locked\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItarget\fP The target to be modified\&.
.br
\fImax_msg_size\fP The new maximum message size for the supplied target\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
The modified target if no error is encountered\&. In the event of an error, NULL is returned and an error code is set appropriately\&. 
.RE
.PP

